# Build context for client/Dockerfile, which CI and the deploy script build as
# `docker build -t <image> client`. Patterns are relative to this directory.
#
# The image COPYs exactly two things — `nginx.conf` and `build/web` — so most of
# what follows only shrinks the context uploaded to the daemon. The first block
# is different: it changes what the image actually serves.

# CanvasKit's symbolication tables (#701). A release `flutter build web` emits
# ~8.6 MB of `canvaskit/*.js.symbols`, and `COPY build/web` would ship and
# publicly serve every byte of it. No browser ever requests them: the service
# worker's precache generator already skips this exact pair of suffixes
# (`kExcludedSuffixes` in client/tool/build_app_shell_cache.dart), so they were
# already understood to be non-assets — they were simply still being copied.
# They are useful only to `flutter symbolize` against a build artifact, which is
# where they remain; nothing needs them resident in a serving container.
#
# Source maps go with them, for the same reason and by the same precedent: never
# fetched by the app, and they hand any reader the unminified sources.
**/*.symbols
**/*.map

# Local dependency trees. None of these are part of the served bundle, but
# without this every `docker build client` uploads whatever a developer happens
# to have installed — `client/e2e` pulls Playwright, and `client/tool/icons`
# pulls a platform-specific Skia binary (@napi-rs/canvas), which is tens of MB
# of pure waste on each build.
**/node_modules/
.dart_tool/
